  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure keeps northern New England dry through the end of\nthe week and the weekend, while an offshore ocean storm brings \nsome cloud cover into parts of the region this week.\n\nWith high pressure in control, another cool night is expected \nwith lows in the 20s to mid 30s. The warmest readings will be \nfound along the coast where an intermittent northerly breeze \nkeeps the airmass more mixed. An inversion around 925mb moves in\naloft during the daytime tomorrow, keeping temps cooler than \ntoday. Additionally, high cloud cover spreads inland from the \ncoastline as the coastal storm drifts closer.\n\nTemps rise a few more degrees ahead of a weak front on \nThursday, before falling back again on Friday. The ocean storm \nmakes its closest pass Wednesday night and Thursday, before \ntracking near the Canadian Maritimes. Behind the departing \nstorm, a fresh Canadian airmass brings widespread lows in the \n20s Friday night.\n\nThe broad high pressure center then drifts southward from the \nend of the week through the weekend. Centered south of New \nEngland, the high brings a moderating trend through the weekend \nand early next week, along with continued dry conditions. Highs \nreturn to the 60s by Sunday, with some low 70s possible by \nMonday next week. A weak cold front is possible by Tuesday next \nweek, but at this point it looks mostly dry as well.",
